● The "buffering headache" file is a session transcript. The session ended mid-investigation before the problem was solved. Here's what it shows and what's likely happening:

  Symptom: Song starts, buffers partway, then data stops flowing. VPN and WiFi both stay up.

  What the logs showed: tun2proxy: Failed to create UDP connection: Protocol not supported by HTTP proxy — these are UDP failures (audio quality updates, telemetry, etc.) but you correctly said those aren't the cause.

  Most likely cause — PdaNet proxy idle timeout on CONNECT tunnels:

  YouTube Music streams in chunks. It downloads ahead of playback, then pauses downloading when its buffer is full. During that pause, the CONNECT tunnel through PdaNet's HTTP proxy sits idle. PdaNet's proxy has an idle timeout — when the tunnel is silent for that period, it closes the connection server-side. When YouTube tries to resume downloading the next chunk, the TCP connection is dead. tun2proxy has to detect that and re-establish, and either it doesn't do that cleanly, or the re-establishment fails silently, so the stream stalls permanently.

  The difference from a regular short HTTP request: those open → transfer → close immediately. Streaming holds a connection open with quiet gaps in between. That idle gap is what kills it.

  The session was interrupted right as the investigation was starting. The last thing Claude was about to do before the interruption was track which specific network started the tunnel and also look at switching DnsStrategy.VIRTUAL to OVER_TCP — but neither of those actually addresses the proxy idle timeout problem, which is the real culprit.

